      "q": "(i) What is a contour line? 2 4 (ii) Define the terms “contour interval” and “horizontal equivalent”. 3 4",
      "answer": "1. CONTOUR LINE DEFINITION:\nA contour line is an imaginary line on the ground surface joining points of equal elevation above a chosen reference datum (Mean Sea Level).\n\n2. KEY TECHNICAL TERMS:\n• Contour Interval: The vertical distance between two consecutive contour lines. It is kept constant for a given map scale and terrain nature.\n• Horizontal Equivalent: The horizontal distance between two consecutive contour lines on the ground. It varies depending on the steepness of the terrain (closely spaced for steep slopes, wide for gentle slopes).\n\n3. CHARACTERISTICS OF CONTOURS:\n• Two contour lines of different elevations do not cross each other, except in the case of an overhanging cliff or cave.\n• Closely spaced contours indicate steep slope; widely spaced contours indicate flat or gentle terrain.\n• Concentric closed contours with higher values inside indicate a hill, while lower values inside indicate a pond or depression.",
