      "answer": "1. CONCEPT OF BEARING:\nThe bearing of a survey line is the horizontal angle between the survey line and a reference meridian.\n\n2. CLASSIFICATION OF MERIDIANS:\n• True Meridian: Geographical line passing through True North and South poles (remains constant).\n• Magnetic Meridian: Direction pointed by a freely suspended, balanced magnetic needle (varies due to declination).\n• Arbitrary Meridian: Convenient line chosen as reference for small local surveys.\n\n3. SYSTEMS OF BEARING:\n• Whole Circle Bearing (WCB): Measured clockwise from North (0° to 360°), as measured by a Prismatic Compass.\n• Reduced / Quadrantal Bearing (QB): Measured from North or South towards East or West (0° to 90°), as measured by a Surveyor's Compass.\n• Fore Bearing (FB) & Back Bearing (BB): BB = FB ± 180° (+ if FB < 180°, - if FB > 180°).",
