      "answer": "1. PURE VIRTUAL FUNCTION & ABSTRACT CLASS:\n• Pure Virtual Function: A virtual function declared in a base class that has no definition and is assigned 0. Syntax: `virtual void display() = 0;`\n• Abstract Class: A class containing at least one pure virtual function. It cannot be instantiated directly and serves as an interface for derived classes.\n\n2. C++ IMPLEMENTATION EXAMPLE:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Abstract Base Class\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual void area() = 0; // Pure virtual function\n};\n\nclass Rectangle : public Shape {\n    int l, b;\npublic:\n    Rectangle(int len, int br) : l(len), b(br) {}\n    void area() override {\n        cout << \"Area = \" << (l * b) << endl;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Shape* s = new Rectangle(5, 4);\n    s->area(); // Output: Area = 20\n    delete s;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n3. ROLE IN RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM:\nEnforces a consistent interface across derived subclasses while allowing late dynamic binding via vtable and vptr.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Exact definition and syntax `virtual void func() = 0;`",
        "Explanation of Abstract Base Class instantiation rules",
        "Complete compilable C++ example illustrating override and polymorphism"
      ],
      "rubric": "Definition & Rules: 2M, Code Example: 2M, Vtable/Polymorphism: 1M"
