      "q": "Explain maximum stress theory, Tsai-Hill, and Tsai-Wu failure criteria for orthotropic composite laminates. State the advantages of Tsai-Wu criteria over Tsai-Hill.",
      "answer": "1. MAXIMUM STRESS CRITERIA:\nAssumes failure occurs when any stress component along principal material axes exceeds the corresponding allowable ultimate strength: σ1 ≥ X_t (or X_c), σ2 ≥ Y_t (or Y_c), |τ12| ≥ S. It ignores stress interaction.\n\n2. TSAI-HILL CRITERIA:\nBased on Von Mises yield criterion generalized for anisotropic materials:\n(σ1/X)² - (σ1·σ2/X²) + (σ2/Y)² + (τ12/S)² = 1.\nIt accounts for stress interactions, but cannot differentiate between tensile and compressive strengths.\n\n3. TSAI-WU TENSOR POLYNOMIAL CRITERIA:\nF_i·σ_i + F_ij·σ_i·σ_j = 1.\nSpecifically: F1·σ1 + F2·σ2 + F11·σ1² + F22·σ2² + 2F12·σ1·σ2 + F66·τ12² = 1.\n\n4. ADVANTAGES OF TSAI-WU:\n• Accounts for differing tensile and compressive strengths (linear terms F1 and F2).\n• Accurate interactive tensor representation matching multi-axial testing data closely.",
