      "group": "Group B",
      "topic": "Relations & Posets",
      "q": "Define equivalence relation and partial order relation. Prove that the 'divides' relation (a | b) on the set of positive integers is a partial order relation.",
      "answer": "1. DEFINITIONS:\n• Equivalence Relation: A binary relation R on set A that is Reflexive, Symmetric, and Transitive.\n• Partial Order Relation (Poset): A relation R on set A that is Reflexive, Antisymmetric, and Transitive.\n\n2. PROOF THAT 'DIVIDES' (a | b) IS A PARTIAL ORDER ON Z⁺:\n• Reflexivity: For any a ∈ Z⁺, a | a because a = a · 1. (Reflexive holds).\n• Antisymmetry: If a | b and b | a, then b = k1·a and a = k2·b for positive integers k1, k2. Then a = k2·(k1·a) = (k1·k2)·a ⇒ k1·k2 = 1. Since k1, k2 ∈ Z⁺, k1 = k2 = 1 ⇒ a = b. (Antisymmetric holds).\n• Transitivity: If a | b and b | c, then b = k1·a and c = k2·b. Substituting gives c = k2·(k1·a) = (k1·k2)·a. Since k1·k2 is an integer, a | c. (Transitive holds).\n\nConclusion: Since 'divides' is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive on Z⁺, (Z⁺, |) is a partially ordered set (Poset).",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Precise definition of equivalence relation and partial order relation",
        "Step-by-step proof of reflexivity, antisymmetry, and transitivity",
        "Rigorous algebraic derivation of a = b in antisymmetry"
      ],
