      "answer": "1. MATRIX VS ELEMENT-WISE OPERATIONS:\n• Matrix Multiplication (`*`): Performs standard inner product row-by-column multiplication. Requires column count of A to equal row count of B.\n• Element-wise Multiplication (`.*`): Multiplies corresponding elements of arrays with identical dimensions.\n\n2. MATLAB CODE EXAMPLES:\n```matlab\nA = [1, 2; 3, 4];\nB = [5, 6; 7, 8];\n\nC_matrix = A * B;    % Matrix product = [19, 22; 43, 50]\nC_elem   = A .* B;   % Element-wise = [5, 12; 21, 32]\n```\n\n3. SOLVING LINEAR SYSTEMS (Ax = b):\nIn MATLAB, the backslash operator (`\\` or mldivide) uses Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting:\n```matlab\nA = [2, 1; 1, 3];\nb = [5; 10];\nx = A \\ b;           % Solves Ax = b, gives x = [1; 3]\n```",
