      "q": "What is operator overloading? Write a complete C++ program to overload the '+' operator to add two complex numbers using member functions.",
      "answer": "1. OPERATOR OVERLOADING CONCEPT:\nOperator overloading allows C++ operators to have user-defined meanings when applied to user-defined data types (classes), enhancing code readability and expressiveness.\n\n2. C++ PROGRAM IMPLEMENTATION:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass Complex {\n    float real, imag;\npublic:\n    Complex(float r = 0, float i = 0) : real(r), imag(i) {}\n\n    // Overload + operator\n    Complex operator + (const Complex& c) const {\n        return Complex(real + c.real, imag + c.imag);\n    }\n\n    void display() const {\n        cout << real << \" + \" << imag << \"i\" << endl;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Complex c1(3.5, 2.5), c2(1.5, 4.5);\n    Complex c3 = c1 + c2; // Equivalent to c1.operator+(c2)\n    cout << \"Result: \";\n    c3.display(); // Output: 5 + 7i\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n3. RULES: Operators like `::`, `sizeof`, `?:`, `.` cannot be overloaded. Arity and precedence remain unchanged.",
