      "q": "Explain how ordinary differential equations (ODEs) are solved in MATLAB using `ode45`. Write a script to solve dy/dt = -2y + sin(t), y(0) = 1 over t ∈ [0, 10] and plot the response.",
      "answer": "1. THE ode45 SOLVER IN MATLAB:\n`ode45` is based on an explicit Runge-Kutta (4,5) formula, the Dormand-Prince pair. It adaptively varies step size to maintain error within specified relative and absolute tolerances (RelTol, AbsTol).\n\n2. COMPLETE MATLAB SCRIPT:\n```matlab\nfunction ode_demo()\n    % Time span and initial condition\n    tspan = [0, 10];\n    y0 = 1;\n\n    % Define ODE function using anonymous function\n    dydt = @(t, y) -2*y + sin(t);\n\n    % Solve using ode45\n    [t, y] = ode45(dydt, tspan, y0);\n\n    % Plotting results\n    figure;\n    plot(t, y, 'b-o', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'MarkerSize', 4);\n    grid on;\n    xlabel('Time t (seconds)');\n    ylabel('Response y(t)');\n    title('Solution of dy/dt = -2y + sin(t) via ode45');\n    legend('Numerical Response y(t)', 'Location', 'Best');\nend\n```\n3. ANALYSIS:\nThe term `-2y` provides stable decay, while `sin(t)` introduces forced steady-state harmonic oscillation.",
