      "group": "Group C",
      "topic": "Bayes' Theorem & Conditional Probability",
      "q": "State and prove Bayes' Theorem. A bolt manufacturing factory has machines A, B, C producing 25%, 35%, and 40% of the total bolts. The defective outputs are 5%, 4%, and 2% respectively. A bolt drawn at random is defective. What is the probability that it was manufactured by machine B?",
      "answer": "1. BAYES' THEOREM STATEMENT:\nIf E1, E2, ..., En are mutually exclusive and exhaustive events, and A is any event with P(A) > 0, then:\nP(Ei | A) = [P(Ei) · P(A | Ei)] / [∑_{k=1}^n P(Ek) · P(A | Ek)].\n\n2. NUMERICAL SOLUTION:\nLet events:\n• B1: Bolt from Machine A ⇒ P(B1) = 0.25\n• B2: Bolt from Machine B ⇒ P(B2) = 0.35\n• B3: Bolt from Machine C ⇒ P(B3) = 0.40\n• D: Drawn bolt is defective.\nGiven conditional probabilities:\n• P(D | B1) = 0.05\n• P(D | B2) = 0.04\n• P(D | B3) = 0.02\n\nTotal Probability P(D):\nP(D) = P(B1)P(D|B1) + P(B2)P(D|B2) + P(B3)P(D|B3)\nP(D) = (0.25 × 0.05) + (0.35 × 0.04) + (0.40 × 0.02)\nP(D) = 0.0125 + 0.0140 + 0.0080 = 0.0345.\n\nProbability that defective bolt came from Machine B:\nP(B2 | D) = [P(B2) · P(D | B2)] / P(D)\nP(B2 | D) = 0.0140 / 0.0345 = 140 / 345 ≈ 0.4058 (or 40.58%).",
