      "answer": "1. NORMAL DISTRIBUTION DEFINITION:\nA continuous random variable X follows a Normal distribution with parameters μ (mean) and σ² (variance) if its PDF is:\nf(x) = [1 / (σ · √(2π))] · exp[- (x - μ)² / (2σ²)], for -∞ < x < ∞.\n\n2. CHIEF PROPERTIES:\n• Bell-shaped, symmetric curve centered at x = μ (Mean = Median = Mode).\n• Total area under curve equals 1. Curve has points of inflection at x = μ ± σ.\n• Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7%):\n  - P(μ - σ < X < μ + σ) ≈ 68.27%\n  - P(μ - 2σ < X < μ + 2σ) ≈ 95.45%\n  - P(μ - 3σ < X < μ + 3σ) ≈ 99.73%.\n• Standard Normal Variable: Z = (X - μ) / σ, where Z ~ N(0, 1).\n\n3. CENTRAL LIMIT THEOREM (CLT):\nLet X1, X2, ..., Xn be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with mean μ and finite variance σ². As sample size n → ∞, the sample mean X̄ approaches a normal distribution N(μ, σ²/n), regardless of the underlying population distribution.\n\n4. PRACTICAL SIGNIFICANCE:\nJustifies using Gaussian models for sensor measurement noise, tolerances in manufacturing, network packet latency distributions, and statistical hypothesis testing.",
